WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.740
A star has been clocked orbiting the Milky Way's central black hole at 25,000 kilometers per second, closer than any previously observed. The star S301, detected with ESOs VLTI, reaches that speed while passing closer to Sagittarius A star than any previously tracked object. Its orbit is tight enough for the black hole's frame-dragging effect to measurably alter the path in coming years. Continued VLTI monitoring will tighten constraints

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:57.560
on the spin parameter. The measurement offers an independent check on earlier event horizon telescope results. Now, shifting to a different scale of orbital challenge, a new pre-print by Hugh G. Lewis at the University of Birmingham warns that current satellite and mega-constallation plans could trigger cascading collisions in low Earth orbit. The analysis models how repeated launches and limited disposal options amplify collision probabilities over decades.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:21.840
Lewis stresses that without stricter end-of-life rules, including mandatory heliocentric disposal and published state vectors, the debris environment could become unmanageable. The paper examines specific growth rates in the latest commercial filings and compares them against existing mitigation guidelines. Follow-up work will test updated disposal scenarios against projected constellation sizes.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:38.480
On a different note, a phased array concept would operate in the radio quiet zone behind the moon. The design targets frequencies blocked or contaminated by Earth's ionosphere and human transmitters, including the still unexplained 1977 Wow signal band.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:55.480
Picture a single large radio dish placed on the moon's far side. Every terrestrial transmitter, every satellite downlink, and even Earth's own ionosphere would sit behind 3500 kilometers of solid rock, creating a silence deeper than any spot on our planet.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:05.420
Radio waves from cosmic sources would arrive unimpeded while human generated noise, which already swamps the Wow signals frequency band today, would be blocked.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:15.040
The proposed array would scan at wavelengths blocked from Earth, listening for faint signals that might reveal the universe's first stars or unknown natural phenomena.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:25.260
Yet the same far side is now targeted for future lunar bases, and each new lander or relay satellite risks injecting its own radio leakage into that protected zone.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:36.720
Scientists still do not know how much interference a single unshielded transmitter would create at these frequencies, nor whether international rules can be written and enforced before construction begins.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.980
The team argues construction must begin before additional lunar infrastructure adds its own interference.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:03:01.540
Next steps include site surveys from upcoming orbiters and prototype antenna tests on Earth analogs. Hubble observations of globular clusters have traced the chemical and orbital signatures of a dwarf galaxy named LKHT that merged with the Milky Way roughly 12 billion years ago.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:08.980
The clusters retain distinct abundance patterns that match the accreted system stars rather than the main galactic population.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:15.020
This evidence strengthens the picture of the Milky Way growing through repeated mergers in its early history.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.760
The same data set is now being cross-checked with Gaia proper motions to map the remnant debris across the halo.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:32.420
Revised stellar mass estimates for early galaxies observed by JWST indicate they contain larger populations of low mass stars than initial models assumed.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:37.900
The adjustment raises total masses and tightens the tension with standard formation timelines.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.300
Multi-wavelength follow-up is planned to refine the initial mass function assumptions used in the reanalysis.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.900
The findings come from reprocessing of the same deep field data sets released earlier this year.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:59.980
Astronomers have identified a roughly 23 Earth mass planet still embedded in its natal disk, far exceeding typical core accretion predictions for its age.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:07.620
The object's density and orbital distance suggest it may have formed through a different channel, possibly involving disk instabilities.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:13.400
Additional radial velocity and transit data are being collected to refine its mass and radius.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:18.080
The discovery adds to a small but growing sample of massive young worlds.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:26.660
The perseverance rover captured a transit of foboes across the Sun from the surface of Mars, producing a brief deep partial eclipse.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:31.700
The event was timed to coincide with the spacecraft's science operations window.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:38.900
Images show the moon's irregular silhouette against the solar disk and will help refine foboes' orbital parameters.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.220
Similar observations are scheduled during future conjunction seasons.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:52.440
Observers across the Americas and Europe will see the moon pass through the outer portion of Earth's umbral shadow on the night of August 27-28.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:59.300
The event completes a rare August sequence that also included a total solar eclipse and the perseid peak.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.580
Maximum eclipse occurs when roughly 94% of the moon's diameter lies inside the umbra.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.060
Timing predictions and visibility maps are already posted for public use.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:20.220
Seven institutions will share approximately $10.5 million to build state-level pathways into skilled technical roles that do not require a bachelor's degree.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:29.160
The new NASA Aerospace Speild Technical Workforce Hub's initiative targets shortages in manufacturing, testing, and operation support.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:36.400
Awards focus on partnerships between community colleges, industry, and state agencies to accelerate training pipelines.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:42.080
Early metrics will track enrollment growth and placement rates into NASA-related contractors.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:52.400
Mogue Incorporated has announced expanded production capacity for its meteorite satellite platform, aimed at high-agility Earth observation and defense payloads.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:57.660
The bus supports rapid orbit changes and precise pointing needed for responsive imaging.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:04.420
New configurations will accommodate larger propellant loads while maintaining the core attitude control architecture.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.980
First flight units are slated for upcoming commercial and government manifests.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:19.260
Texas A&amp;M researchers used virtual reality environments plus added smell and touch cues to simulate long-duration isolation experienced by sailors.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:26.000
The work aims to identify sensory interventions that could mitigate psychological strain on future Mars missions.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.680
Early results show measurable drops in reported stress when multiple senses are engaged.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.140
Follow-on trials will test similar setups with astronaut analogs.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:46.460
A new analysis warns that US industrial capacity is lagging behind the combined demand from commercial constellations, national security programs, and civil missions.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.600
Key pressure points include specialty electronics, propulsion components, and qualified launch site personnel.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:58.880
The report recommends targeted investments in domestic suppliers and workforce training.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.440
Without action, schedule slips and cost overruns are projected to worsen.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:15.540
A US Air Force aircraft heading to Antarctica reversed course on August 18th after receiving a space debris hazard alert tied to unspecified Russian operations over the South Pacific.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:21.500
The incident highlights how even routine polar flights now intersect with growing orbital traffic.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.560
Officials have not released the exact object or altitude involved.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.540
Similar alerts are expected to increase as launch rates rise.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:37.420
The first astronomer royal began systematic observations that laid groundwork for accurate stellar catalogs used in navigation.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:44.240
Despite leaving school early, Flamsteed taught himself the mathematics needed to compute planetary positions.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:49.420
His work directly addressed the longitude problem that plagued 17th century sailors.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:55.780
Modern historians continue to study his notebooks for insights into early telescope calibration techniques.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:03.660
The article profiles emerging leadership and partnership models NASA is adopting to expand exploration beyond traditional centers.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:09.220
It highlights how smaller institutions and private firms are being integrated into mission planning.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:14.740
Specific examples include technology demonstrations slated for upcoming Artemis flights.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:20.300
The piece notes that these shifts aimed to broaden the industrial base supporting human space flight.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:27.820
Keep an eye on upcoming VLTI observations that could refine the spin measurement of Sagittarius A star in the months ahead.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:30.480
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

